 "answer": "**Linking CSS.** Use a `<link>` element in the `<head>`:\n\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\">\n\nOther ways: a `<style>` block in the head (internal), or the `style` attribute on an element (inline). External files are best: cacheable and shared across pages. `@import` inside CSS also works but is slower.\n\n**Navigating between tabs.** The question is ambiguous, so cover both readings:\n- *Browser tabs:* links open in a new tab with `target=\"_blank\"`, and you should add `rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"` so the new page cannot access `window.opener`: `<a href=\"page.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open</a>`.\n- *Keyboard focus order:* the Tab key moves focus through links, buttons and inputs. Control it with `tabindex` (`0` follows the natural order, `-1` is focusable only by script; avoid positive values).\n- *UI tabs on a page:* build them with buttons and panels using ARIA roles (`role=\"tablist\"`, `role=\"tab\"`, `role=\"tabpanel\"`) and a little JavaScript, or anchor links to section ids.\n\n**Dates in HTML.** Use the native input, which gives a calendar picker and validation:\n\n    <input type=\"date\" min=\"2025-01-01\" max=\"2025-12-31\">\n\nRelated types: `time`, `datetime-local`, `month`, `week`. The value format is `YYYY-MM-DD`. Appearance varies by browser, and a text fallback or a library may be needed in older ones. The `<time datetime=\"2025-05-16\">` element marks up a date semantically.\n\n**`<canvas>`.** An element that provides a bitmap drawing surface controlled by JavaScript, via the 2D context (or WebGL for 3D):\n\n    <canvas id=\"c\" width=\"300\" height=\"150\"></canvas>\n    <script>\n      const ctx = document.getElementById(\"c\").getContext(\"2d\");\n      ctx.fillRect(10, 10, 100, 50);\n    </script>\n\nUsed for charts, games, image manipulation and animation. Unlike SVG, drawn shapes are pixels, not DOM elements, so they are not individually selectable or accessible; provide fallback text inside the tag.\n\n**Lists.** `<ol>` is an ordered list (numbered; order matters, such as steps); `<ul>` is an unordered list (bullets; order does not matter). Both contain `<li>` items, and the `type` and `start` attributes or CSS `list-style` change markers.",
